[node name="RichTextLabel" type="RichTextLabel" parent="PanelContainer/VBoxContainer/TabContainer/Overview" unique_id=1894314812]
layout_mode = 2
theme_override_fonts/normal_font = ExtResource("5_5vlgg")
bbcode_enabled = true
text = "[b][font_size=24]Welcome to Space Colony Cheeses II - The Computer Game[/font_size][/b]

The colonisation of space is going smoothly. Spaceships fly on schedule from colony to colony, bearing the imports and exports of dozens of planets. But most of the trade is centred on necessities. There's no way to send a tasteful Christmas gift of assorted cheeses and beef sausages to a loved one on another planet. You've been assigned to change all that.

You are in charge of colonising a newly discovered world, Bos IV. Bos IV is similar in most ways to Bos III (see the [b]Space Colony Cheeses[/b] board game) but you [b][color=red]cannot[/color][/b] build Space Ports here (the natives won’t allow it!) and it has less sunlight, so you [b][color=red]cannot[/color][/b] use Solar Power! Instead the boffins have perfected [b]Portals[/b] for travelling to Bos III (which does have Space Ports) and they have also discovered that Bos IV has an almost unlimited quantity of [b]Yapthalene Crystals[/b] (which come in four funky colours - Green, Orange, Sky Blue & Yellow) that can be grown (and mined) in special areas called [b]Crystal Mines[/b]. Your task is to build a thriving, profitable, dairy colony using these crystals for power, as well as geothermal energy from certain areas of the planet (anywhere you can see steam rising from the ground is where you can place your [b]Geothermal Powerplants[/b]). You must choose when, where and how to expand. You'll have to balance the need for money-making against the need for energy production. If you can afford it, you may want to expand into other dairy products, increase your storage capacity, or just harass the competition with endless lawsuits. The choices, and the responsibility, are yours.

Finally, last year saw one of the biggest NanoTech breakthroughs in history - the creation of a special breed of Cow (the Nano-Cow). These Nano-Cows are invisible to the human eye, but can be milked and slaughtered using special machinery. To populate your pastures with these amazing creatures, you firstly build a [b]Pasture Dome[/b], then you release the special Nano-Fluid onto the crystals and, in a few short minutes, there will literally be billions of Nano-Cows living and breeding in your [b]Pasture Dome[/b] and spreading to your [b]Barns[/b], with nothing for you to do but ensure the modules are maintained and, of course, reap the rewards.

[b]Space Colony Cheeses II[/b] is a light-hearted, non-violent game for 2-4 players, based on [b]Space Colony Cheeses[/b], a board game by [b]The Mad Game Designer (aka Michael Fischer)[/b] - http://www.cke1st.com/m_games1.htm."

[node name="Introduction" type="MarginContainer" parent="PanelContainer/VBoxContainer/TabContainer" unique_id=1572988759]
visible = false
layout_mode = 2
theme_override_constants/margin_left = 25
theme_override_constants/margin_top = 5
theme_override_constants/margin_right = 25
metadata/_tab_index = 1

[node name="RichTextLabel" type="RichTextLabel" parent="PanelContainer/VBoxContainer/TabContainer/Introduction" unique_id=1358383209]
layout_mode = 2
size_flags_horizontal = 3
size_flags_vertical = 3
theme_override_fonts/normal_font = ExtResource("5_5vlgg")
bbcode_enabled = true
text = "[b][font_size=24]I. Introduction[/font_size][/b]

The ultimate goal of the game is to make money ($). You do this by adding modules to your colony. Some modules generate $, and some [color=Red]do not[/color], but all can be useful to the colony. Each player begins the game with $100.

You roll a dice to see who goes first. Each colony MUST begin with a [b]Start-Up[/b] module. A colony can [b]only[/b] be started in one of the four (4) coloured quadrants of the board. Only [b]one[/b] player can start in any one (1) quadrant and a player may [b][color=red]not[/color][/b] start multiple colonies.

[b][font_size=24]II. The Map[/font_size][/b]

This is unlike most maps you will have seen. There are two kinds of grid units, [b]squares[/b] and [b]octagons[/b]. A square has only four neighbours, while an octagon has eight. This can be important when arranging your modules.

The map has two kinds of features, [b]Geothermal Steam[/b] and [b]Lush Pastures[/b]. [b]Geothermal Steam[/b] can be found anywhere that you see steam rising from the ground and are the [b]only[/b] places that you can build [b]Geothermal Powerplants[/b]. [b]Lush Pastures[/b] have grass growing on them, and give a bonus to any [b]Barns[/b] that are built on them.

[b][font_size=24]III. Building Colonies[/font_size][/b]

Bos IV is an anomaly. Earth-type plants grow well here, but the atmosphere does [b][color=red]not[/color][/b] retain oxygen. Thus, there is plenty of grassland for your Nano-Cows to munch on, but everything must be enclosed in modular domes with artificial atmospheres.

All of these modules must be connected to each other. Only the [b]Start-Up[/b] module can stand alone. Each module requires 1 energy unit per turn. Modules that are energy-short [b][color=red]cannot[/color][/b] function and produce nothing. You can, however, build a module with no energy source, and add one later.

Food is not a problem for your colony, since that is exactly what you are producing. As your colony makes commodities, they are kept track of on the scoreboard, as is your current bank balance."

[node name="Game Sequence" type="MarginContainer" parent="PanelContainer/VBoxContainer/TabContainer" unique_id=1049106894]
visible = false
layout_mode = 2
theme_override_constants/margin_left = 25
theme_override_constants/margin_top = 5
theme_override_constants/margin_right = 25
metadata/_tab_index = 2

[node name="RichTextLabel" type="RichTextLabel" parent="PanelContainer/VBoxContainer/TabContainer/Game Sequence" unique_id=1429837122]
layout_mode = 2
theme_override_fonts/normal_font = ExtResource("5_5vlgg")
bbcode_enabled = true
text = "[b][font_size=24]IV. Game Sequence[/font_size][/b]

1. Colonies with a [b]Construction module[/b] can move any one of their own modules currently placed on the map. Such a move will cost half of the original cost of the module.

2. Each colony can add up to a maximum of 3 new modules, if it has enough $ in the bank and space on the map.

3. Each colony rolls a dice. If it comes up a 1, then a new disease has started in the herds of that colony (yes, Nano-Cows can get sick as well). One of the [b]Barn[/b] (or [b]Megabarn[/b] modules of that colony [b]must[/b] now be marked as [b]sick[/b]. That [b]Barn[/b] (or [b]Megabarn[/b] is now unable to produce anything until the disease is cured. A colony [b][color=red]cannot[/color][/b] have more than 4 diseases at once, so any others that crop up are ignored. If the colony has 4 diseases at one time, then it [b][color=red]cannot[/color][/b] sell anything except [b]Leather[/b] and [b]Manure[/b].

4. Each colony calculates its energy production and then subtracts the energy needs of all the other modules. If there is [b][color=red]not[/color][/b] enough energy to go around, modules [b]must[/b] be marked as [b]offline[/b] (which denotes an energy shortage) until there is enough energy available to power them again (i.e. marked as [b]online[/b]). An energy-producing module cannot be [b]offline[/b], and any surplus energy is lost.

5. Each colony now calculates its commodity production from each [b]Barn[/b] (or [b]Megabarn[/b] next to a [b]Processing Plant[/b], and allocates it to the eight available commodities - [b]Milk[/b], [b]Cream[/b], [b]Butter[/b], [b]Ice Cream[/b], [b]Cheese[/b], [b]Beef[/b], [b]Leather[/b] and/or [b]Manure[/b]. A colony [b][color=red]cannot[/color][/b] make more of a particular commodity than it has storage for (the maximum storage capacity of any product is 10). If there is [b][color=red]no[/color][/b] storage space available for a commodity, then it [b][color=red]cannot[/color][/b] be made.

6. Each colony now rolls two dice, and a [b]random event[/b] is generated. The colony [b]must[/b] follow all instructions for the [b]random event[/b]. Note that should the colony be allowed to change a commodity price, then the change will be applied in Step 8. Also note that commodity prices can never fall below 0.

7. Each colony that has a functioning [b]Portal[/b], a functioning [b]Store[/b], and at least one stored commodity of [b]Cheese[/b] and one of [b]Beef[/b], can now sell [b]gift boxes[/b] (see [b]Store[/b]). If a [b]gift box[/b] is sold, then the stored amounts of [b]Cheese[/b] and [b]Beef[/b] will be reduced accordingly.

8. A dice is now rolled for each commodity to set the price for the current turn. Each colony with a functional [b]Portal[/b] can now sell as many of that commodity as they want, adjusting the price by the results of their [b]random event[/b] roll as needed.

9. Now, all income from [b]Start-Ups[/b], [b]Cheese Labs[/b], and [b]Sausage Labs[/b] are added to the bank balance of the colony, and [b]Law Offices[/b] file lawsuits if required.

10. A dice is now rolled for each diseased [b]Barn[/b] or [b]Megabarn[/b]. If it comes up 5 or 6, then the disease has been [b]cured[/b]. Note that two dice rolls are allowed if the colony has a [b]Veterinary[/b] module. If the disease is cured, [b]Barn[/b] (or [b]MegaBarn[/b]) is no longer marked as [b]sick[/b].

11. Finally, all stored commodities, except for [b]Leather[/b] and [b]Manure[/b], are reset to zero."

